-loc><publisher-name>United Nations/Public Policy Development Office</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="R21"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Chadwick</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name></person-group><source>Report from the Poor Law Commissioners on an Inquiry into the Sanitary Conditions of the Laboring Population of Great Britain</source><year>1842</year><publisher-loc>London</publisher-loc><publisher-name>HMSO</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="R22"><citation citation-type="gov"><person-group person-group-type="author"><collab>The Office of Population Censuses and Surveys</collab></person-group><source>Census 1841-1851</source><year>1851</year><publisher-loc>London</publisher-loc><publisher-name>HMSO</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="R23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Szreter</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The Population Health Approach in Historical Perspective</article-title><source>Am J of Pub Health</source><year>2003</year><volume>93</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>421</fpage><lpage>431</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12604486</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R24"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Morris</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name></person-group><source>Cholera, the Social Response to an Epidemic</source><year>1976</year><publisher-loc>New York</publisher-loc><publisher-name>Holmes &#x00026; Meier</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="R25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gill</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Cholera and the Fight for Public Health Reform in Mid-Victorian England</article-title><source>Historian</source><year>2000</year><volume>66</volume><fpage>10</fpage><lpage>16</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="R26"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Snow</surname><given-names>SJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Sunderland, Snow and Water: The Transmission of Cholera in the Nineteenth Century</article-title><source>Int Journal of Epidemiology</source><year>2002</year><volume>31</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>908</fpage><lpage>911</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="R27"><citation citation-type="journal"><article-title>150 Years of Cholera Epidemiology</article-title><source>The Lancet</source><year>2005</year><volume>366</volume><issue>9490</issue><fpage>957</fpage></citation></ref><ref id="R28"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hennock</surname><given-names>EP</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The Urban Sanitary Movement in England and Germany, 1838-1914</article-title><source>Continuity and Change</source><year>2000</year><volume>15</volume><issue>2</issue><fpage>269</fpage><lpage>296</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="R29"><citation citation-type="confproc"><source>Report of the House of Commons Select Committee on the Health of Towns</source><conf-date>1840 June 17</conf-date></citation></ref><ref id="R30"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Susser</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Bresnahan</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Origins of Epidemiology</article-title><source>Annals of the NY Academy of Sciences</source><year>2001</year><volume>954</volume><fpage>6</fpage><lpage>18</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="R31"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hamlin</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Sheard</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Revolutions in Public Health: 1848, and 1998?</article-title><source>BMJ</source><year>1998</year><volume>317</volume><fpage>587</fpage><lpage>591</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9721121</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R32"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fee</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Brown</surname><given-names>TM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The Public Health Act of 1848</article-title><source>Bulletin of WHO</source><year>2005</year><volume>83</volume><issue>11</issue><fpage>866</fpage><lpage>867</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="R33"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Calman</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The 1848 Public Health Act and its Relevance to Improving Public Health in England Now</article-title><source>BMJ</source><year>1998</year><volume>317</volume><fpage>596</fpage><lpage>598</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9721123</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R34"><citation citation-type="confproc"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Slaney</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name></person-group><source>Hansard's Parliamentary Debates, House of Commons</source><conf-date>1848 Feb. 10</conf-date></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>George Cruikshank's 1829 cartoon <italic>London Going Out of Town or The March of Bricks and Mortar!</italic> depicted the city's outward expansion.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="yjbm_80_2_61_g01"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p><bold>Very ill!</bold> An early 1800s illustration (artist unknown) depicting the widespread suffering of the working classes. The gaunt individual is drinking tea to help repel an illness. Conventional medicine was unaffordable to the poor at that time.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="yjbm_80_2_61_g02"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>A late 1820s view of London's water: "Monster Soup commonly called Thames Water, being a correct representation of that precious stuff doled out to us!"</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="yjbm_80_2_61_g03"/></fig><fig id="F4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Louis-L&#x000e9;opold Boilley's Consultation de M&#x000e9;dicine 1760 (left) and Consultation de M&#x000e9;dicine 1823 (right, published mid-1820s). In pre-revolution France, the doctors are shown to be old and dazed. In the later era, the doctors are shown in a prime of life and using a scientific approach to the study of health.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="yjbm_80_2_61_g04"/></fig><fig id="F5" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>George Cruikshank's <italic>Cholera Consultation</italic> (1832) illustrates the ineffective and dishonest nature of health authorities. In the image, a board member is offering a toast: "May we preserve our health by bleeding the country."</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="yjbm_80_2_61_g05"/></fig><fig id="F6" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>A sketch from the early 1830s of a victim of cholera in Sunderland (source: Wellcome Library, London).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="yjbm_80_2_61_g06"/></fig><fig id="F7" position="float"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>British public health pioneers. Top (from left): Edwin Chadwick and Sir James Kay-Shuttleworth. Bottom: Neil Arnott.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="yjbm_80_2_61_g07"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>